<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aga of Kish</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agga of Kish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> was a king of Kish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>, around the twenty-seventh century BCE, listed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> as the last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Kish and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmebaragesi">Enmebaragesi</a>. He is known chiefly from the Sumerian poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> and Aga, which tells how he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and was captured and then released by Gilgamesh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aga-of-kish.jpg" alt="Aga of Kish" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aga_of_Kish_Stele_of_Ushumgal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
